   <dc:creator>
    <rdf:Seq>
     <rdf:li>Heeju Je</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>Seoyoung Choi and Beom Kwon</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Seq>
   </dc:creator>
   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">A GT 3D Bounding-Box-Based Framework for Geometric Quantification and Spatial Validation of Vehicle Frontal Blind Spots</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">A GT 3D Bounding-Box-Based Framework for Geometric Quantification and Spatial Validation of Vehicle Frontal Blind Spots</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>3D bounding box; blind spot; roadside perception; spatial validation; vehicle visibility</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Vehicle-induced frontal blind spots can restrict a driver's direct visibility of pedestrians and other vulnerable road users</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Vehicle-induced frontal blind spots can restrict a driver's direct visibility of pedestrians and other vulnerable road users</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and their spatial extent depends on vehicle geometry and driver-viewpoint conditions. Conventional approaches based on category-level representative dimensions may not preserve geometric variation among individual vehicles</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and their spatial extent depends on vehicle geometry and driver-viewpoint conditions. Conventional approaches based on category-level representative dimensions may not preserve geometric variation among individual vehicles</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">whereas detailed visibility models typically require vehicle-specific computer-aided design models and driver accommodation information. This study proposes a geometric framework that uses ground-truth (GT) three-dimensional (3D) bounding boxes from the TUMTraf roadside perception dataset as an intermediate representation for frontal blind-spot quantification and spatial placement. Vehicle-specific GT width is used to estimate the blind-spot ground area and volume</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">whereas detailed visibility models typically require vehicle-specific computer-aided design models and driver accommodation information. This study proposes a geometric framework that uses ground-truth (GT) three-dimensional (3D) bounding boxes from the TUMTraf roadside perception dataset as an intermediate representation for frontal blind-spot quantification and spatial placement. Vehicle-specific GT width is used to estimate the blind-spot ground area and volume</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">while bounding-box position</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">while bounding-box position</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">dimensions</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">dimensions</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and yaw are used to spatially anchor the resulting geometry. A mean-width baseline comparison</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and yaw are used to spatially anchor the resulting geometry. A mean-width baseline comparison</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">sensitivity analyses of representative visibility parameters</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">sensitivity analyses of representative visibility parameters</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and calibration-based camera projection were conducted to evaluate complementary aspects of the framework. Replacing vehicle-specific GT widths with the mean-width baseline resulted in a mean absolute percentage error of 5.8 and a maximum relative error of 38.3. Increasing the assumed frontal obstruction height from 1.030 to 1.160 m increased the estimated blind-spot distance from 8.583 to 21.091 m</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and calibration-based camera projection were conducted to evaluate complementary aspects of the framework. Replacing vehicle-specific GT widths with the mean-width baseline resulted in a mean absolute percentage error of 5.8 and a maximum relative error of 38.3. Increasing the assumed frontal obstruction height from 1.030 to 1.160 m increased the estimated blind-spot distance from 8.583 to 21.091 m</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">demonstrating substantial sensitivity to visibility assumptions. In an exploratory spatial validation of 27 vehicles</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">demonstrating substantial sensitivity to visibility assumptions. In an exploratory spatial validation of 27 vehicles</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">all three assessors classified 24 cases (88.9) as Valid or Partially Valid</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">all three assessors classified 24 cases (88.9) as Valid or Partially Valid</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">although agreement on individual four-category verdicts was moderate (Fleiss' =0.512). These qualitative results describe the evaluated sample and do not establish population-level performance or physical blind-spot accuracy. The findings support the feasibility of using GT 3D bounding boxes as a compact geometric representation for vehicle-level blind-spot analysis while highlighting the need for vehicle-specific visibility parameters and independent physical validation.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">although agreement on individual four-category verdicts was moderate (Fleiss' =0.512). These qualitative results describe the evaluated sample and do not establish population-level performance or physical blind-spot accuracy. The findings support the feasibility of using GT 3D bounding boxes as a compact geometric representation for vehicle-level blind-spot analysis while highlighting the need for vehicle-specific visibility parameters and independent physical validation.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
